{"seq":1,"ts":"2026-11-05T04:41:17.060Z","type":"exp.start","exp":"E-AUCTION-PROGRAMS-01","attempt":1,"title":"Sealed-bid auction rules the Roost can run: first-price, second-price (Vickrey) and uniform-price, and what the Roost will sign for them","tier":"offline","hypothesis":"Each format gives the textbook outcome on worked examples and keeps its invariants over thousands of random bid sets: charges sum exactly to the seller's proceeds, no member pays more than they bid, the highest bid wins, a uniform auction fills from the top and charges everyone the same price, and the same bids always give the same outcome. Under second-price, bidding your true value is never worse than any other bid (tested against rivals drawn at random); under first-price it is not (a case where shading a bid wins more is found). The Roost signs only the outcome it computed: altered charges, altered allocations, charges on an ordinary aggregate, and a second signature are all refused, and the signed message has the 7-field charged format. Fails if any invariant breaks, if truthful bidding is ever beaten under second-price, or if the Roost signs anything other than its own outcome.","limits":["The rules are the standard textbook ones; this tests that the code implements them and that the Roost cannot be made to sign anything else. It does not test that these are the right rules for any real sale.","Bids are not authenticated to a NEAR account: whoever submits a sealed bid names the flyerId. An impersonator could grief another member (GAP-023). The contract still caps any charge at that member's deposit and the Roost caps it at the bid.","The strategy tests use random rivals and a grid of alternative bids; they support the theory, they are not a proof.","The Roost is a local HostedRoost with a simulated attestation provider."],"requires":[],"chain_prev":"4c6d8a4e1420d77cf7499c387990a737d6b917809eaf648553df01cbd8327e67","chain_hash":"6896b772e291c8bc22905d02e547da03e986dbbb948a2811b720d09dc9a32854"}
